.\" ai-tools-project-registry(5) -- an operator's project registry for Agent Tools Restricted.
.\" @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@ is substituted at deploy time (install.sh install_subst
.\" and the RPM %prep), like ai-tools(1). The grammar described here is the one
.\" conf.lib.sh implements for the registry, shared by every reader.
.\" tests/unit/man.sh validates each EXAMPLES block holding entries as a file
.\" and classifies each example line whose comment opens with a state word.
.TH AI-TOOLS-PROJECT-REGISTRY 5 "" "ai-tools @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@" "Agent Tools Restricted"
.SH NAME
ai-tools-project-registry \- the project directories an operator's agent sessions may start in
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ~/.config/ai-tools/project-registry
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each operator keeps one project registry, and it decides two things for that
operator:
where a session launched by that account may start, and which agent-written
paths the ownership hand-back returns to that account.
A session starts only inside a project the file lists, and only where no park
or carve-out line covers the directory.
.PP
The file is a launch gate and an ownership registry, not a read boundary.
Once a session is running, ordinary file permissions and the SELinux policy
decide what it reads and writes, as
.BR ai-tools (1)
states.
.PP
The file is owned by the operator at mode
.BR 0600 ,
inside a
.B 0700
directory, so no other account reads it.
.B "atr admin operators add"
creates it with the format marker, a header and no entry, so no session can
start anywhere until a project is claimed.
The
.B "atr projects"
commands of
.BR ai-tools (1)
add, park, restore and remove entries in place.
Edit the file by hand to review it, to comment it, or to carve a subtree out
of a project.
.SH FORMAT
The format marker is the first line
.B "atr admin operators add"
and
.B "atr admin system post-upgrade"
write:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# ai-tools-project-registry-format: 1.0
.EE
.RE
.PP
The marker is a comment spelled exactly so, with one space after the colon
and the version as two decimal numbers.
A reader takes the first marker in the file.
This release reads format 1.
A minor version it does not know is read, since every line is checked on its
own;
a major version other than 1 refuses the file.
.SS The file an earlier release wrote
A release before format 1.0 kept the registry in
.IR ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects ,
with allow and
.B !\&
lines and no marker.
While that file is present it stays the registry:
sessions start as before, and every
.B "atr projects"
command but
.B "atr projects list"
refuses until the file is migrated.
.PP
.B "atr admin system post-upgrade"
migrates it, unattended included.
It writes
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/project-registry
as the marker followed by every line unchanged, at the old file's owner and
mode, and then renames the old file to
.IR ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects.YYYYMMDD-N.bak .
The run prints each line first.
It names every
.B !\&
line no project encloses: in that file such a line may have parked a
project, and in format 1.0 it is a carve-out.
Change its
.B !\&
to
.B %
to keep it as a parked project that
.B "atr projects enable"
restores.
.SH GRAMMAR
One entry per line.
The first character of a line is its kind, written before any quote.
.TP
.I /home/op/project
An allow line: a project.
Sessions may start in the directory and in every directory under it.
.TP
.I !/home/op/project/vendor
A carve-out: the agent is withheld from the directory and everything under
it.
The line means that wherever it sits: inside a project, around one, or alone.
A carve-out may be a glob: a line carrying
.BR * ,
.B ?
or
.B [
matches the whole path as written,
.RB \(oq !/home/op/*/node_modules \(cq.
A carve-out is not a project:
no
.B "atr projects"
command claims, parks, restores or deletes it, and none deletes the line.
.TP
.I %/home/op/parked
A park: a project taken out of service.
No session starts there, and the entry, the permissions and the SELinux label
stay in place.
.B "atr projects disable"
writes it and
.B "atr projects enable"
lifts it.
A park may name a project nested inside another one.
.TP
.I "/home/op/project   # why"
A comment.
A
.B #
at the start of a line, or after whitespace, begins one.
.TP
.I "\(dq/home/op/ai works\(dq"
One layer of matched double or single quotes carries a path containing a
space or a literal
.BR # .
The kind character precedes the quote,
.RB \(oq "%\(dq/home/op/ai works\(dq" \(cq,
and only a comment may follow the closing quote.
.PP
Blank lines are ignored.
An allow line is resolved to its real path before it is compared, so an entry
reached through a symbolic link or a trailing slash names the directory.
A line naming a directory that no longer exists does not start a session, and
.B "atr projects list"
reports it as stale.
.PP
A park or carve-out covers the path as written and, where every symbolic link
on the way is one the sandbox account can neither remove, replace nor move,
its real path as well:
the link and every directory on the way to it are yours or root's, and none of
those directories is writable by group or other, a sticky directory such as
.I /tmp
aside.
A link inside a claimed project fails that test, since the sandbox account
writes the project.
.SH VALIDITY
A line the grammar refuses refuses the whole file, for every reader:
no session starts under any entry in it, the hand-back does not act on any
path it names, and every
.B "atr projects"
command refuses.
The refusal,
.BR MSG-B8U2 ,
names the file, the line number, the reason, the line with any control
character shown as
.BR ? ,
and the edit that fixes it.
The reasons are:
.TP
.B unknown-form
The line starts with a character other than
.BR / ,
.BR \(dq ,
.BR \(aq ,
.BR ! ,
.B %
or
.BR # .
.TP
.B relative-path
The path is not absolute.
.TP
.B empty-entry
A
.B !\&
or
.B %
with no path, or empty quotes.
.TP
.B unmatched-quote
A quote is not closed.
.TP
.B text-after-quote
Text other than a comment follows the closing quote.
.TP
.B control-character
The path carries a control character, or the file carries a NUL byte on any
line, a comment included.
.TP
.B glob-on-allow
An allow line carries a glob character.
List each project by its path.
.TP
.B glob-on-park
A park carries a glob character.
A park names one project.
.TP
.B park-in-legacy
A
.B %
line in an
.I allowed-projects
file, whose grammar does not have one.
.TP
.B marker-in-legacy
A format marker in an
.I allowed-projects
file.
.TP
.B marker-missing
A
.I project-registry
file without a marker.
.TP
.B marker-repeated
A second marker.
.TP
.B marker-unsupported
A marker naming a major version other than 1.
.PP
A park or carve-out spelled through any other symbolic link refuses the file
the same way, under
.BR MSG-Y5N6 ,
which names the entry, the link and the real path to write in its place.
.PP
.B "atr status"
and
.B "atr admin status"
report the file as refused, and
.B "atr admin system post-upgrade --check"
writes a
.B registry-invalid
finding for it.
.SH ENTRY STATES
Every
.B "atr projects"
command reads one state per path from the lines of the file:
.TP
.B listed
An allow line names the path.
.TP
.B parked
A park names the path.
An allow line beside it is a duplicate, which
.B "atr projects enable"
folds into one line.
.TP
.B carve-out
A carve-out names the path, and no other line does.
.TP
.B conflict
A carve-out and an allow or park line name the same path.
The path is neither a project nor a carve-out, and every command that acts on
it refuses until one of the lines is deleted.
.TP
.B inside
No line names the path, and a project encloses it.
.TP
.B ancestor
No line names the path or encloses it, and projects sit under it.
.TP
.B unregistered
None of these.
.PP
A path is reachable, so a session may start there and the hand-back acts
there, when it is
.BR listed ,
or
.B inside
a listed project, and no park, carve-out or glob line covers it.
A listed project inside a parked one is listed and not reachable.
A command refused over a path names the line that blocks it, and a command
that changes an entry names any other line that still blocks the path.
.PP
While a project is parked, the hand-back does not return files written under
it, so park a project once its sessions have ended.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/project-registry
This file, one per operator.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects
The registry an earlier release wrote, read in its place until
.B "atr admin system post-upgrade"
migrates it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects.YYYYMMDD-N.bak
That file as the migration read it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/secret-patterns
The operator's secret-name patterns, kept beside it with the same ownership.
.TP
.I /var/opt/ai-tools/sandbox-projects/
The sandbox-clone area; a clone made by
.B "atr projects clone"
is listed here like any other project.
.SH EXAMPLES
A project, a carve-out inside it, a project nested in it, and two parked
projects, one of them nested:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# ai-tools-project-registry-format: 1.0
/home/op/webapp             # listed: sessions start here
!/home/op/webapp/vendor     # carve-out: third-party code
/home/op/webapp/tools       # listed, inside webapp
%/home/op/webapp/legacy     # parked, inside webapp
%/home/op/oldapp            # parked: projects enable restores it
.EE
.RE
.PP
A path containing a space, a quoted carve-out inside it, and a glob carve-out
of every dependency directory one level under another project:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# ai-tools-project-registry-format: 1.0
"/home/op/ai works"         # listed
!"/home/op/ai works/build"  # carve-out
/home/op/webapp
!/home/op/webapp/*/node_modules
.EE
.RE
.PP
Register a project, review the file, and park and restore a project without
editing it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr projects claim ~/src/webapp
atr projects list
atr projects disable ~/src/oldapp
atr projects enable ~/src/oldapp
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ai\-tools (1),
.BR ai\-tools\-admin (8),
.BR ai\-tools\-operator.conf (5),
.BR ai\-tools\-secret\-patterns (5)
.PP
The lifecycle guide covers claiming a tree in place against working in a
sandbox clone, and what each prompt grants:
.IR /usr/share/doc/ai\-tools/docs/projects/index.md .
